['Letters to Cleo'] {'response': 'The provided context is insufficient regarding the number of members in Letters to Cleo. However, it does provide information about Screaming Trees, which was formed in 1985 and originally consisted of four members: vocalist Mark Lanegan, guitarist Gary Lee Conner, bassist Van Conner, and drummer Mark Pickerel. Later, Mark Pickerel was replaced by Barrett Martin, bringing the total number of members to five during their most successful period. Without information about Letters to Cleo, I cannot determine which band had more members.', 'query': 'Which band, Letters to Cleo or Screaming Trees, had more members?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': 'The discography of Screaming Trees, an Ellensburg, Washington-based rock band, consists of seven studio albums, three compilation albums, five extended plays (EP), and six singles, though this does not include any solo material recorded by the individual members of Screaming Trees.', 'node_id': '0fcfe32d-dd4f-4420-bc15-b92030909cf9', 'score': 9.429105758666992}, {'text': "Screaming Trees was an American rock band formed in Ellensburg, Washington in 1985 by vocalist Mark Lanegan, guitarist Gary Lee Conner, bass player Van Conner and drummer Mark Pickerel. Pickerel had been replaced by Barrett Martin by the time the band reached its most successful period. Although widely associated with grunge, the band's sound incorporated hard rock and psychedelic elements. During Screaming Trees' existence the band released seven studio albums, five EPs, and three compilations.", 'node_id': 'dc845a66-4297-4e9e-bc85-aea5bfa4c315', 'score': 8.493975639343262}, {'text': 'Beat Happening/Screaming Trees is an EP and a one-off collaboration between Beat Happening (from Olympia, Washington) and Screaming Trees (from Ellensburg, Washington). Originally released on Homestead Records in 1988, it was later reissued as part of Beat Happening\'s box set "Crashing Through" in 2002. The journal of Kurt Cobain contains a draft of a letter sent to Mark Lanegan, in which he described "Polly Pereguin" as his favorite pop song of the 1980s.', 'node_id': 'afe17b56-4575-4d8d-b178-e1e85c582d0e', 'score': 8.483055114746094}, {'text': "Gary Lee Conner (born Lee Gary Conner on August 22, 1962 in Fort Irwin, California, USA) was the lead guitarist for rock act Screaming Trees, along with his brother Van Conner, who was the band's bassist. Conner began his career with Screaming Trees in 1985 which lasted until their disbanding in 2000.", 'node_id': '4dce05c9-b708-49a3-b5f4-73c5dd30c7d9', 'score': 8.4138765335083}, {'text': 'Van Conner (born March 17, 1967 in Apple Valley, California) is an American rock musician. In 1984 he co-founded the band Screaming Trees with his brother Gary Lee Conner and close friends Mark Lanegan and Mark Pickerel. On the 25 June 2000 Screaming Trees announced their official breakup. Conner would start a low key band under the name Gardener which released its only album in the 1990s under the name "New Dawning Time".', 'node_id': 'f0eb87e0-6f39-4571-b181-eafc2d490e9e', 'score': 7.80451774597168}, {'text': 'Mad Season was an American rock supergroup formed in Seattle, Washington in 1994 by members of three popular Seattle-based bands: Alice in Chains, Pearl Jam and Screaming Trees. Mad Season released only one album, "Above", and is best known for the single "River of Deceit". The band went on a semi-permanent hiatus in 1996 due to the band members\' conflicting schedules and vocalist Layne Staley\'s problems with substance abuse.', 'node_id': 'c10dcd36-e864-432f-aeee-7a7ea88f17b4', 'score': 7.505970001220703}, {'text': "Mark Pickerel is an American musician best known as the original drummer for the Screaming Trees, a rock group from Ellensburg, Washington, that formed in 1985. Other members of the band included the versatile singer Mark Lanegan and the Conner brothers. Pickerel played on the band's extended play and first five albums and left the group in 1992 and was replaced by Barrett Martin.", 'node_id': 'c7d3d031-0117-4ac6-bc7c-a020782d541b', 'score': 7.3365654945373535}, {'text': 'Truly is an American rock band formed in the wake of the grunge era. It featured singer-guitarist Robert Roth, bassist Hiro Yamamoto, and drummer Mark Pickerel. Yamamoto and Pickerel were founding members respectively of Soundgarden and Screaming Trees. While not a commercially successful group like some of their Seattle contemporaries, the band lasted a decade with two studio albums to their name.', 'node_id': '40d21130-8b56-4bfd-9a45-5b6ec209ed0e', 'score': 7.1913909912109375}, {'text': 'Change Has Come was the only recording the Screaming Trees released through Sub Pop. Released in 1990 the Screaming Trees then moved on to a major label, Epic Records, for their next three albums - "Uncle Anesthesia", "Sweet Oblivion," and "Dust", as well as the "Something About Today" EP.', 'node_id': '522507ba-ed5e-467d-aa3d-803fbe0fc965', 'score': 6.742462158203125}, {'text': 'In computer science, a Fractal Tree index is a tree data structure that keeps data sorted and allows searches and sequential access in the same time as a B-tree but with insertions and deletions that are asymptotically faster than a B-tree. Like a B-tree, a Fractal Tree index is a generalization of a binary search tree in that a node can have more than two children. Furthermore, unlike a B-tree, a Fractal Tree index has buffers at each node, which allow insertions, deletions and other changes to be stored in intermediate locations.', 'node_id': 'cd833d53-38cc-41f0-acad-6bbb89050711', 'score': 5.762621879577637}], 'consistency_check': 'CONFLICT', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'CONTRADICTION', 'response': 'ENTAILMENT'}, 'latency': 1.3497383929789066, 'vram_usage': 0.0}
