['Usher'] {'response': 'The provided context is insufficient regarding the name of the singer whose song was released as the lead single from the album "Confessions" and that had a popular song stuck behind for eight consecutive weeks.', 'query': 'What is the name of the singer who\'s song was released as the lead single from the album "Confessions", and that had popular song stuck behind for eight consecutive weeks?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': 'The album was preceded by the release of the lead single "Missing You" on 5 August 2010. It became the group\'s seventh top-ten hit when it peaked at number three in the UK and number six in Ireland.', 'node_id': '4248ee19-6b69-4694-ba9c-1d5c6b1048d3', 'score': 1.0585761070251465}, {'text': 'On the album Rise and Fall, Rage and Grace, it appears as the third track after Trust in You.', 'node_id': '5ed0974c-93ae-4777-929c-8d509bb3947d', 'score': 1.0651755332946777}, {'text': '"Numb" is a song by Barbadian singer Rihanna from her seventh studio album "Unapologetic" (2012). It features guest vocals by American rapper Eminem, making it the pair\'s third collaboration since the two official versions of "Love the Way You Lie". Following the album\'s release, "Numb" charted on multiple charts worldwide including in Canada, the United Kingdom and the United States.', 'node_id': 'eca235b0-b152-455f-8461-e8559d35cef6', 'score': 1.0693376064300537}, {'text': 'Long was also featured on The Notorious B.I.G.\'s hit song "Hypnotize", singing the chorus.', 'node_id': '3b54b392-f5d3-4f7f-aeb9-001674ea5735', 'score': 1.0829287767410278}, {'text': 'It peaked at #30 on the Australian singles charts. A remix version by [love] tatto was released on the Icehouse album "Meltdown" in 2002.', 'node_id': 'cef91e47-a727-48db-818c-5d52d3d2c0d0', 'score': 1.1149320602416992}, {'text': 'A single had been originally slated to be released in November 2009 with an album release in February 2010, but the band went back into the studio in January 2010. According to lead singer and guitarist John Rzeznik, this was done to make further improvements on what they had previously thought had been a finished record. Several producers were brought in to assist on the production process, including Butch Vig, John Fields, Paul David Hager and Rob Cavallo.', 'node_id': '52686d9d-cf2c-4e69-839d-db3aba7a146d', 'score': 1.117199420928955}, {'text': 'Another song taken from the album, the Daryl Hall and Janna Allen-penned "Method of Modern Love" reached a high point of #5, and "Some Things Are Better Left Unsaid," reached #18.', 'node_id': '73f38200-7434-4242-9471-3740813d59b3', 'score': 1.1276917457580566}, {'text': 'A single and video was released for the track "Short Attention Span".', 'node_id': '6b1ddb28-29fd-4053-85f4-3c8f5aa9878e', 'score': 1.131188154220581}, {'text': 'With "Fast" also reaching number one in April 2017, Bryan became the first artist in the chart\'s history to achieve six number one singles from one album.', 'node_id': '494ce776-3fbb-47df-9d52-2ed613bb0951', 'score': 1.1411188840866089}, {'text': '"Always" is the first single off the album "Back into Your System" by the rock band Saliva with lead vocalist Josey Scott. It made it to number 1 on the Modern Rock Tracks for one week in February 2003, and has been Saliva\'s most successful hit, peaking at #51 on the Hot 100.', 'node_id': '57ba0b97-248e-43e0-afc1-c983d47d4e27', 'score': 1.1414344310760498}, {'text': 'His third solo album, "Ollusion" (2010), was released on January 12, 2010, with the lead single, "I Get It In". His fourth solo album, "Sex Playlist" (2014), spawned the single, "Post to Be", which was certified 3x platinum and reached number 13 on the Hot 100 chart in May 2015, and became his highest charting single to date.', 'node_id': 'c13cd8b7-89e6-4dc4-bd77-a0e8c3632c29', 'score': 1.1454908847808838}, {'text': 'In preparation for its release, the single was remixed to feature new vocals from American rapper Flo Rida after the rapper\'s single "Club Can\'t Handle Me" beat the group\'s previous single "Missing You" to number one; it was the second time they had lost the position to Flo Rida. Rochelle Wiseman jokingly said that the group would never get a number one until they collaborated with him.', 'node_id': 'de0d52e3-2f22-4bc9-8672-4b2afe680cab', 'score': 1.1464393138885498}, {'text': '"The Proud One" is a 1966 single written by Bob Gaudio and Bob Crewe and originally performed by Frankie Valli and The Four Seasons, and credited to Valli solo; that version peaked at #68.', 'node_id': 'dbb570c8-3855-4758-93ce-d6144222edc2', 'score': 1.1564795970916748}, {'text': '"Be the One" is a power ballad by American rock band Poison. The song was released as the second single from their 2000 "Crack a Smile...and More!" album. The single/sampler was released in February 2000 and featured five b-sides including the first single from the album "Shut Up, Make Love" and the four unplugged bonus tracks from the album: "Every Rose Has Its Thorn", "Talk Dirty to Me", "Your Mama Don\'t Dance" and "Something to Believe In".', 'node_id': '8c5fcc1f-7045-4e51-99b0-60cf00510b5f', 'score': 1.1610853672027588}, {'text': 'This would be Astley\'s last record with Stock, Aitken & Waterman, due to Astley wanting to leave behind the dance-pop sound of the producers, of wanting to shed his boy next door image, and wanting to focus on his original compositions on his albums, reappearing in 1991 with the soul album "Free".', 'node_id': '755d5c09-8628-4379-9969-6960a457dbd8', 'score': 1.1661884784698486}, {'text': "Produced by Brian Virtue (Jane's Addiction, Velvet Revolver, Thirty Seconds to Mars, Chevelle) and Rick Parashar (Pearl Jam, Alice in Chains, 3 Doors Down, Nickelback), the album features a half-dozen freshly penned tracks and six from the band's 2009 Australian CD, Nowhere Left to Hide, remixed by Dan Korneff (My Chemical Romance, Cavo).", 'node_id': 'd23b5908-3bc4-4e39-8950-461fe3d8df5c', 'score': 1.1669020652770996}, {'text': 'It remains Winans\' only major success to date. As a result of its eight weeks at number 2, "I Don\'t Wanna Know" stands in joint fifth place for the most weeks at number 2 by a song which did not go to number 1, behind Foreigner\'s "Waiting for a Girl Like You", Missy Elliott\'s "Work It", Donna Lewis\'s "I Love You Always Forever" and Shania Twain\'s "You\'re Still the One".', 'node_id': '5d5c22e9-58e3-487d-a715-e0f395412b51', 'score': 1.169400691986084}, {'text': 'Old 8×10 is the third studio album by country music star Randy Travis. It was released on July 12, 1988 by Warner Bros. Records Nashville. The album produced the singles "Honky Tonk Moon", "Deeper Than the Holler", "Is It Still Over", and "Promises". All of these except "Promises" reached Number One on the Hot Country Songs charts in the late 1980s. The German edition of the album (the first Randy Travis release in that country) contained the bonus track "Forever and Ever, Amen".', 'node_id': '7802d248-557d-4105-a73f-0bb9cad0a714', 'score': 1.169893741607666}, {'text': 'The album does contain, however, her version of "Stop" for the first time on a Jamelia album.', 'node_id': '9a9d5a5f-9c62-4ca0-bfe5-60882fd83a01', 'score': 1.1722524166107178}, {'text': '"The Songs That We Sing" was #78 on "Rolling Stone"\' s list of the 100 Best Songs of 2007.', 'node_id': '16411272-2c9e-432d-a3de-27dc02cf95f3', 'score': 1.172645926475525}], 'consistency_check': 'MISSING', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'CONTRADICTION', 'response': 'ENTAILMENT'}, 'latency': 4.003185223788023, 'vram_usage': 1.44677734375}
