 45%|████▌     | 225/500 [38:02<1:06:10, 14.44s/it]['French Canadians'] {'response': "The ethnic group that settled in Canada in the 17th century and participated in a conflict with Irish Catholic immigrants in the Shiners' War is the French Canadians. The Shiners' War, which took place in Bytown from 1835 to 1845, involved Irish Catholic immigrants, led by Peter Aylen, attacking French Canadian timber operations and engaging in street fights with them. This conflict highlights the tensions between these two groups during that period in Canadian history.", 'query': "What is the name of the ethnic group who settled in Canada in the 17th century that participated in a conflict with Irish Catholic immigrants in the Shiners' War?", 'retrieved_context': [{'text': 'The Shiners\' War was a conflict between Irish Catholic immigrants and French Canadians in Bytown from 1835 to 1845. The war started when Peter Aylen, a major Irish timber operator, organized a group of Irishmen to attack other timber operations. This group was known as the "Shiners." They attacked French Canadian timber rafts and fought against the French Canadian on the streets of Bytown.', 'node_id': '08970de1-b69f-4347-b2b6-be27fab03be0', 'score': 0.4700937867164612}, {'text': 'Irish Canadians (Irish: "Gaedheal-Cheanadaigh" ) are Canadian citizens who have full or partial Irish heritage including descendants who trace their ancestry to immigrants who originated in Ireland. 1.2 million Irish immigrants arrived from 1825 to 1970, and at least half of those in the period from 1831–1850. By 1867, they were the second largest ethnic group (after the French), and comprised 24% of Canada\'s population.', 'node_id': 'e7617fd8-f463-4392-9817-d6ca994438c1', 'score': 0.7669247388839722}, {'text': 'In the 1840s and 50s, the anti-Catholic, anti-immigrant Know-Nothing movement targeted Irish Catholics in Boston. In the 1860s, many Irish immigrants fought for the Union in the American Civil War, and that display of patriotism helped to dispel some of the prejudice against them.', 'node_id': '489e5482-98fe-4f6d-b23a-181d059fd00d', 'score': 0.8871190547943115}, {'text': 'The 1931 national census counted 1,230,000 Canadians of Irish descent, half of whom lived in Ontario. About one-third were Catholic in 1931 and two-thirds Protestant.', 'node_id': 'df7ff79d-3a32-4398-8590-971b09f89e33', 'score': 1.0069966316223145}, {'text': 'People of Irish descent form the largest ethnic group in the city of Philadelphia and its surrounding counties. The Irish have lived in Philadelphia since the pre-American Revolution period. Irishmen had participated in pro-Revolutionary activities in Philadelphia during the Revolutionary War. Like many American cities in the 19th century, Philadelphia, which was once a Quaker stronghold, changed dramatically with the influx of European immigrants. The first major influx of Irish came in 1844 from rural areas, spurred by the Irish Famine.', 'node_id': '8c7baac7-2df9-4b7b-9a1b-fd2f2f17764e', 'score': 1.0281190872192383}], 'consistency_check': 'CONSISTENT', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'CONTRADICTION', 'response': 'NEUTRAL'}, 'latency': 1.035532634705305, 'vram_usage': 1.39990234375}
