the 1930s until it was outlawed by the U.S. Supreme Court\'s decision in "United States v. Paramount Pictures, Inc." (1948). Under block booking, "independent (\'unaffiliated\') theater owners were forced to take large numbers of [a] studio\'s pictures sight unseen.', 'node_id': 'ea0c77e6-9a07-48d2-8626-1341af34a6c2', 'score': 0.9989838004112244}, {'text': 'Reese" (1875), "Reynolds v. United States" (1878), "Wilkerson v. Utah" (1879), the "Trade-Mark Cases" (1879), "Strauder v. West Virginia" (1880), "Pace v. Alabama" (1883), "United States v. Harris" (1883), "Ex parte Crow Dog" (1883), "Hurtado v. California" (1884), "Clawson v. United States" (1885), "Yick Wo v. Hopkins" (1886), "United States v.', 'node_id': '010de086-a0f6-436d-bb67-f89177e8f069', 'score': 1.0846302509307861}, {'text': 'The Marshall Court (1801–1835) heard forty-one criminal law cases, slightly more than one per year. Among such cases are "United States v. Simms" (1803), "United States v. More" (1805), "Ex parte Bollman" (1807), "United States v. Hudson" (1812), "Cohens v. Virginia" (1821), "United States v. Perez" (1824), "Worcester v. Georgia" (1832), and "United States v. Wilson" (1833).', 'node_id': 'f74d8bfb-8b50-4e9f-b7f7-7d833da4c284', 'score': 1.119300127029419}], 'consistency_check': 'MISSING', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'NEUTRAL', 'response': 'ENTAILMENT'}, 'latency': 1.2241705525666475, 'vram_usage': 1.27099609375}
