['Donna Paige Helmintoller'] {'response': 'The American actress and singer born in 1956 who recorded the song "Something There" for "Beauty and the Beast" is Paige O\'Hara. She voiced the character Belle in the film, and the song is performed during a scene where Belle and the Beast begin to acknowledge their feelings for each other. Paige O\'Hara\'s performance is part of the film\'s main cast, which also includes other notable actors such as Robby Benson as the Beast, Jerry Orbach as Lumiere, Angela Lansbury as Mrs. Potts, and David Ogden Stiers as Cogsworth.', 'query': 'What american actress/singer born in 1956 recorded the song Something there for beauty and the beast?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': '"Something There" is a song written by lyricist Howard Ashman and composer Alan Menken for Walt Disney Pictures 30th animated feature film "Beauty and the Beast" (1991). Sung by the majority of the film\'s main cast, the song was recorded by American actors Paige O\'Hara as Belle and Robby Benson as the Beast via voice over, featuring actors Jerry Orbach, Angela Lansbury and David Ogden Stiers as Lumiere, Mrs. Potts and Cogsworth, respectively.', 'node_id': '83623af8-41e3-4c89-a1af-eb1b711535bd', 'score': 0.7427352666854858}, {'text': 'Donna Paige Helmintoller, better known as Paige O\'Hara (born May 10, 1956), is an American actress, singer and painter. O\'Hara began her career as a Broadway actress in 1983 when she portrayed Ellie May Chipley in the musical "Showboat". In 1991, she made her motion picture debut in Disney\'s "Beauty and the Beast", in which she voiced the film\'s heroine, Belle.', 'node_id': '102538eb-a666-4910-88af-28ba7fa7fbb5', 'score': 0.852806806564331}, {'text': '"Beauty and the Beast" was subsequently recorded as a pop duet by Canadian singer Celine Dion and American singer Peabo Bryson, and released as the only single from the film\'s soundtrack on November 25, 1991.', 'node_id': '3ae5338f-8c28-47e5-a743-002730f49689', 'score': 0.8649693727493286}, {'text': 'Potts and Cogsworth, respectively. The only song performed by the Beast, "Something There" is heard midway through "Beauty and the Beast "during a scene in which Belle and the Beast finally begin to acknowledge their own feelings for each other.', 'node_id': 'c88e7ff5-cc64-4214-9393-66185641b46c', 'score': 0.8783913850784302}, {'text': '"Belle" is a song written by composer Alan Menken and lyricist Howard Ashman for Walt Disney Pictures\' 30th animated feature film "Beauty and the Beast" (1991). Originally recorded by American actress and singer Paige O\'Hara and American actor Richard White, "Belle", a mid-tempo French and classical music-inspired song, incorporates both Broadway and musical theatre elements.', 'node_id': 'f7c20a72-ba68-4876-9667-e42707d84529', 'score': 0.9564476013183594}, {'text': '"Evermore" is a song written by composer Alan Menken and lyricist Tim Rice for the musical fantasy film "Beauty and the Beast" (2017), a live-action remake of Disney\'s 1991 animated film of the same name. Originally recorded for the film by English actor Dan Stevens, who performs the song in his starring role as the titular Beast, "Evermore" was first released as a single by American singer Josh Groban on March 3, 2017.', 'node_id': '290e7b77-91ab-41c9-91d1-bd0b9ef46703', 'score': 0.9695932865142822}, {'text': 'Anna Simpson (born March 23, 1985) is an American actress and singer born in Brooklyn, New York. She is best known for the film, "Our Song", alongside Kerry Washington and Melissa Martinez (2000). She has a daughter named Chasity who she gave birth to at the age of 15. Simpson is a survivor of sexual abuse.', 'node_id': 'a95eada7-400b-48bb-b403-8eee89b0e5ba', 'score': 1.0033035278320312}, {'text': '"Beauty and the Beast" is a song written by lyricist Howard Ashman and composer Alan Menken for the Disney animated feature film "Beauty and the Beast" (1991). The film\'s theme song, the Broadway-inspired ballad was first recorded by British-American actress Angela Lansbury in her role as the voice of the character Mrs. Potts, and essentially describes the relationship between its two main characters Belle and the Beast, specifically how the couple has learned to accept their differences and in turn change each other for the better.', 'node_id': '5f907052-98ff-42d9-b959-48e66c34e4a4', 'score': 1.0082762241363525}, {'text': 'Following the critical and commercial success of "Beauty and the Beast", O\'Hara reprised her role as Belle in the film\'s two direct-to-video follow-ups, "" and "Belle\'s Magical World".', 'node_id': 'bdceb9b0-cc90-48f7-b191-1e1b200da1e0', 'score': 1.037986397743225}, {'text': 'The film\'s first song and opening number, "Belle" appears during "Beauty and the Beast "as a large scale operetta-style production number that introduces the film\'s heroine Belle, considered a book-loving nonconformist by the townspeople of the village, who has grown weary of the provincial life in which she is supposed to live, and Gaston, the film\'s narcissistic villain who wishes to desire her hand in marriage despite Belle\'s rejections.', 'node_id': '61355bf3-af39-48af-83f1-535b05391580', 'score': 1.0484752655029297}], 'consistency_check': 'CONSISTENT', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'CONTRADICTION', 'response': 'ENTAILMENT'}, 'latency': 1.6338113881647587, 'vram_usage': 1.24755859375}
