 59%|█████▉    | 295/500 [48:33<39:47, 11.65s/it]['Continental Army'] {'response': 'The namesake of the ship launched as the München in 1930, General von Steuben, fought in the Continental Army during the American Revolutionary War. He was a German officer who played a significant role in training American troops, helping to improve their discipline and effectiveness in battle. His contributions were crucial to the success of the Continental Army in its fight for independence from British rule.', 'query': 'What army did the namesake of the ship launched as the München in 1930 fight in during the American Revolutionary War?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': 'SS "General von Steuben was a German passenger liner and later an armed transport in the Kriegsmarine of Nazi Germany that was sunk during World War II. She was launched as München (sometimes spelled Muenchen), renamed in 1930 as General von Steuben (after the famous German officer of the American Revolutionary War), and renamed again in 1938 as Steuben".', 'node_id': '3d13eb62-2efb-41a5-a7e9-d70a22884378', 'score': 0.8597538471221924}, {'text': 'Two ships of the United States Navy have been named Belleau Wood, after the Battle of Belleau Wood near Château-Thierry in France.', 'node_id': 'c2f6bb80-bad3-41a8-a337-723ee40d8173', 'score': 1.0588884353637695}, {'text': 'USS "Belleau Wood was a United States Navy "Independence"-class light aircraft carrier active during World War II in the Pacific Theater, from 1943 to 1945. The ship also served in the First Indochina War under French Navy temporary service as Bois Belleau".', 'node_id': '340dd90c-a11b-4f9e-a990-43ec45da9026', 'score': 1.065040946006775}, {'text': 'Z43 was a Type 1936B destroyer of Nazi Germany\'s "Kriegsmarine". She was laid down on 1 May 1942 at Deschimag in Bremen, launched on 22 September 1943 and commissioned on 24 March 1944. Her service was with the 6th Destroyer Flotilla in the Baltic Sea under the command of Kapitän zur See Wenniger and later Fregattenkapitän Lampe.', 'node_id': '4d6fdd20-23a5-4413-a61f-e09ea3828ebd', 'score': 1.0878348350524902}, {'text': 'Paris was the third ship of the "Courbet"-class battleship s, the first dreadnoughts built for the French Navy. She was completed before World War I as part of the 1911 naval building programme. She spent the war in the Mediterranean, spending most of 1914 providing gunfire support for the Montenegrin Army until her sister ship "Jean Bart" was torpedoed by the submarine "U-12" on 21 December. She spent the rest of the war providing cover for the Otranto Barrage that blockaded the Austro-Hungarian Navy in the Adriatic Sea.', 'node_id': '9402bd3c-e368-42b0-8892-9ba231536e8e', 'score': 1.112499475479126}], 'consistency_check': 'CONSISTENT', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'ENTAILMENT', 'response': 'NEUTRAL'}, 'latency': 1.0900296475738287, 'vram_usage': 1.28271484375}
