 first volume of Psyren was released in the US by Viz Media's Shonen Jump.", 'node_id': '0e011a53-c015-4db5-bdbc-3888b877095d', 'score': 1.103139877319336}, {'text': 'The first women\'s magazine was published in Malaysia in 1932. In the 2000s there were nearly fifty local titles addressing women in the country. These magazines also include those having an Islamic perspective. Some international women\'s magazines are also published in Malaysia. One of them is "Elle Malaysia", which was first published in March 2014. Another one, "Women’s Health", was started in April 2015. Comic magazines and film magazines are also common in Malaysia.', 'node_id': '4fb66193-7f6e-4f63-b796-1e5a0b410860', 'score': 1.1404776573181152}, {'text': "Magazines in Portugal are mostly women's magazines, society magazines and TV magazines. In 1994 there were nearly 984 magazines in the country.", 'node_id': '79f14f79-996d-4247-85b3-ac708bd55402', 'score': 1.1646358966827393}, {'text': "It features serialized chapters from four manga series, and articles on Japanese language and culture, as well as manga, anime, video games, and figurines. Prior to the magazine's launch, Viz launched an extensive marketing campaign to promote it and help it succeed where previous manga anthologies published in North America had failed. Shueisha purchased an equity interest in Viz to help fund the venture, and Cartoon Network, Suncoast, and Diamond Distributors became promotional partners in the magazine.", 'node_id': '6e2f2ea0-5411-4fa7-8cd5-fac3cdd16f16', 'score': 1.1677751541137695}, {'text': "The following is an incomplete list of current and defunct magazines published in Egypt. They may be published in Arabic or in other languages. The history of Egyptian magazines is long, dating back to the 1890s. The earliest magazines also included women's magazines as well as those published in Turkish from 1828 to 1947. The first children's magazine was published in 1893.", 'node_id': '66522916-bf12-43dd-ac01-e10c917031a5', 'score': 1.1696040630340576}, {'text': 'A total of 379 chapters were serialized, spanning 42 volumes. As of volume 40, the manga has sold over 40 million copies in Japan. News that a sequel to the manga series was going to be developed was announced in the December issue of the Japanese manga magazine "Jump Square". The new manga series, entitled "New Prince of Tennis", began serialization in the "Jump Square" magazine on March 4, 2009, with the story taking place several months after the end of the original manga. Viz Media acquired the license to distribute the series in English in North America.', 'node_id': '4b00a630-fcae-45a2-a229-6de4a3a8026a', 'score': 1.1783084869384766}, {'text': 'index Magazine was a prominent New York City based publication with in-depth interviews with prominent figures in art and culture. It was created by Peter Halley and Bob Nickas in 1996, running until late 2005.', 'node_id': '16ad55d4-7014-4db2-9b81-2e981409e7dd', 'score': 1.200529932975769}], 'consistency_check': 'MISSING', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'NEUTRAL', 'response': 'ENTAILMENT'}, 'latency': 1.122913246974349, 'vram_usage': 1.18896484375}
