 63%|██████▎   | 317/500 [51:06<27:23,  8.98s/it]['Oldham County'] {'response': "Oldham County is the Kentucky county that has a population of 60,316, as recorded in the 2010 census. It is also the county that features the Lake Louisvilla neighborhood, which is located between Westport Road in Louisville and KY 22 in Oldham County. Oldham County's county seat is La Grange, and it was previously a prohibition or completely dry county until January 2005, when it became a wet county following a vote in late 2015.", 'query': 'What Kentucky county has a population of 60,316 and features the Lake Louisvilla neighborhood?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': 'Lake Louisvilla is a neighborhood partially located in Louisville, Kentucky. It is located between Westport Road in Louisville and KY 22 in Oldham County. Lake Louisvilla was developed in the 1920s as a summer resort for people living in the city of Louisville. The state of Kentucky drained the lake in the late 1980s due to safety concerns regarding the stability of a dam.', 'node_id': 'c71ffb11-45b2-4958-bcd5-3a4a524fc1ea', 'score': 0.4770174026489258}, {'text': "Kentucky County (then alternately spelled Kentucke County) was formed by the Commonwealth of Virginia by dividing Fincastle County into three new counties: Kentucky, Washington, and Montgomery, effective December 31, 1776. During the three and one-half years of Kentucky County's existence, its seat of government was Harrodstown (then also known as Oldtown, later renamed Harrodsburg).", 'node_id': '23239e5c-5ef8-4114-a12b-37ba1abe02ec', 'score': 0.8942357301712036}, {'text': "Oldham County is a county located in the commonwealth of Kentucky. As of the 2010 census, the population was 60,316. Its county seat is La Grange. The county is named for Colonel William Oldham. Oldham County was a prohibition or completely dry county until January 2005 as the result of a 2004 'moist' vote, permitting sales of alcohol in restaurants that seat at least 100 patrons in which 70%+ of total revenue is derived from sales of food. After a vote in late 2015; Oldham county has become a completely wet county.", 'node_id': '84135df2-366c-4acb-a354-c5e099c5d227', 'score': 1.063342571258545}, {'text': 'Casey County is a county located in the U.S. Commonwealth of Kentucky. As of the 2010 census, the population was 15,955. Its county seat is Liberty. The county was formed in 1806 from the western part of Lincoln County and named for Colonel William Casey, a pioneer settler who moved his family to Kentucky in 1779. It is the only Kentucky county entirely in the Knobs region. Casey County is home to annual Casey County Apple Festival, and is a prohibition or dry county. It is considered part of the Appalachian region of Kentucky.', 'node_id': '1ef1503f-b26f-4f0c-9afa-bcb73a9b2f82', 'score': 1.1009994745254517}, {'text': 'Shawnee State Fishing Lake is approximately eight miles northwest of Topeka, Kansas, and is in northern Shawnee County, Kansas. This lake is sometimes confused with Lake Shawnee, on the east side of Topeka. It is a 135 acre surface area lake with a maximum depth of 25 ft . This is a manmade freshwater lake, which was constructed in the late 1960s. The lake is widest at the dam, which is at the south end of the lake. It can be reached by a number of roads.', 'node_id': '1ae8f93a-d221-44a0-b308-44a767946d8c', 'score': 1.1433467864990234}], 'consistency_check': 'CONSISTENT', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'CONTRADICTION', 'response': 'CONTRADICTION'}, 'latency': 1.197727084159851, 'vram_usage': 1.24755859375}
