 67%|██████▋   | 334/500 [55:18<48:01, 17.36s/it]['1964'] {'response': 'Reynolds v. Sims was decided first. It was a United States Supreme Court case that was decided in 1964, establishing the principle of "one man, one vote" in state legislative elections. On the other hand, Selle v. Gibb was decided later, in 1984, by the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit. Therefore, Reynolds v. Sims predates Selle v. Gibb by two decades.', 'query': 'Which case was decided first, Selle v. Gibb or Reynolds v. Sims?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': 'Reynolds v. Sims, 377 U.S. 533 (1964) was a United States Supreme Court case that ruled that unlike in the election of the United States Senate, in the election of "any" chamber of a state legislature the electoral districts must be roughly equal in population (thus negating the traditional function of a State Senate, which was to allow rural counties to counter balance large towns and cities). The case was brought on behalf of voters in Alabama by M.O.', 'node_id': 'df1c8ede-5d21-4885-81ff-7909453e7d52', 'score': 0.8702659606933594}, {'text': 'The case was brought on behalf of voters in Alabama by M.O. Sims, a taxpayer in Birmingham, Alabama, but affected both northern and southern states that had similarly failed to reapportion their legislatures in keeping with changes in state population after its application in five companion cases in Colorado, New York, Maryland, Virginia, and Delaware.', 'node_id': '28e7651b-3c15-46aa-9c91-9107db68495e', 'score': 0.9538986086845398}, {'text': 'Selle v. Gibb, 741 F.2d 896 (7th Cir. 1984) was a landmark ruling on the doctrine of striking similarities. The U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit ruled that while copying must be proved by access and substantial similarity, where evidence of access does not exist, striking similarities may raise an inference of copying by showing that the work could not have been the result of independent creation, coincidence, or common source. Striking similarity alone is not enough to infer access. The similarity must preclude independent creation in order to infer access.', 'node_id': '473f5f6e-dc74-41d6-97e4-2935748adf51', 'score': 1.0505664348602295}, {'text': 'Charles "Chuck" Morgan Jr. (March 11, 1930 – January 8, 2009) was an American civil rights attorney from Alabama who played a key role in establishing the principle of "one man, one vote" in the Supreme Court of the United States decision in the 1964 case "Reynolds v. Sims" and represented Julian Bond and Muhammad Ali in their legal battles.', 'node_id': 'ec9b65ac-8da8-45ca-85e9-412be6de02d5', 'score': 1.1232969760894775}, {'text': 'Reynolds v. Pegler, 223 F.2d 429 (2nd Cir. 1955), was a landmark libel decision in which Quentin Reynolds successfully sued right-wing columnist Westbrook Pegler, resulting in a record judgment of $175,001.', 'node_id': '2b0521b3-0b23-4518-b725-d007f7515cc3', 'score': 1.1486268043518066}], 'consistency_check': 'MISSING', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'NEUTRAL', 'response': 'ENTAILMENT'}, 'latency': 1.069844899699092, 'vram_usage': 1.23583984375}
