['no'] {'response': 'The provided context is insufficient regarding Sorbaria. However, it does mention that Solidago, which is commonly known as goldenrod, is a genus of flowering plants in the sunflower family. The context specifies that Solidago contains multiple species, but it does not provide an exact number of species within the genus. Therefore, I cannot confirm whether Solidago has more than 50 species based on the provided information. Thus, the answer to your question is no, as the context does not support the claim that both Sorbaria and Goldenrod are genera of more than 50 flowering plants.', 'query': 'Both Sorbaria and Goldenrod are a genus of more than 50 flowering plants?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': 'Brintonia is a monotypic genus of flowering plants in the sunflower family, containing the single species Brintonia discoidea. It is known commonly as the rayless mock goldenrod. It is native to the southeastern United States, where it is distributed in Alabama, Florida, Georgia, Louisiana, and Mississippi.', 'node_id': 'd6476924-33d5-4e70-ae85-283c0e6c210e', 'score': 9.926647186279297}, {'text': 'Ehretia is a genus of flowering plants in the borage family, Boraginaceae. It contains about 50 species. The generic name honors German botanical illustrator Georg Dionysius Ehret (1708–1770).', 'node_id': '8cb46626-13eb-4781-a99f-5a03210a175a', 'score': 9.920997619628906}, {'text': "Solidago curtisii, commonly called Curtis' goldenrod and mountain decumbent goldenrod, is a North American species of flowering plants in the sunflower family. It is the eastern part of the United States from Pennsylvania to Mississippi and Alabama, primarily in the southern Appalachian Mountains.", 'node_id': 'f7816d1f-82ee-4a1a-805a-f4f9845a4f90', 'score': 8.693735122680664}, {'text': "Bigelowia nuttallii (Nuttall's rayless goldenrod) is a species of North American flowering plants in the daisy family, native to the southern United States (Texas, Louisiana, Alabama, Georgia, and Florida).", 'node_id': '24ff0ed8-e1fd-4fdc-9b9a-6a238d066157', 'score': 8.312517166137695}, {'text': 'Solidago rugosa, the wrinkleleaf goldenrod or rough-stemmed goldenrod, is a North American species of flowering plants in the sunflower family. It is widespread across eastern and central Canada (from Newfoundland to Ontario) and the eastern and central United States (Maine west as far as Wisconsin and Iowa, south to Florida and Texas).', 'node_id': '76024c7f-c2a6-419a-b442-b8dd3fa4c140', 'score': 8.171955108642578}, {'text': 'Bigelowia nudata (pineland rayless goldenrod) is a species of North American flowering plants in the daisy family, native to the coastal plain of the southeastern United States (from eastern Louisiana to North Carolina).', 'node_id': 'b4b76aea-b855-4693-9a23-9dd6ece3310e', 'score': 8.088573455810547}, {'text': 'The genus Huernia (family Apocynaceae, subfamily Asclepiadoideae) consists of stem succulents from Eastern and Southern Africa, first described as a genus in 1810. The flowers are five-lobed, usually somewhat more funnel- or bell-shaped than in the closely related genus "Stapelia", and often striped vividly in contrasting colours or tones, some glossy, others matt and wrinkled depending on the species concerned. To pollinate, the flowers attract flies by emitting a scent similar to that of carrion.', 'node_id': '4087b38c-7654-446e-8215-5f8f76e00569', 'score': 8.051321029663086}, {'text': 'Solidago rupestris , the rock goldenrod or riverbank goldenrod, is a North American species of flowering plants in the sunflower family. It is found in the eastern United States, found today in the States of Maryland, Virginia, Kentucky, and Tennessee. There are historical records of it formerly growing in Indiana and Pennsylvania as well, but these populations now appear to have been extirpated.', 'node_id': 'c3b93d02-4d13-47b1-bda9-83b063cccd70', 'score': 7.934553146362305}, {'text': 'Silene tomentosa, the Gibraltar campion, is a very rare flowering plant of the genus "Silene" and the family Caryophyllaceae. It is a woody-based perennial about 40cm high, with bilobed flowers ranging from pink to pale violet and is endemic to Gibraltar.', 'node_id': '592757fb-108d-4ff8-93a0-ec3ed866fbd0', 'score': 7.748763561248779}, {'text': 'Solidago ouachitensis is a North American species of flowering plants in the aster family known by the common name Ouachita Mountain goldenrod. It has a very limited range, found only in the Ouachita Mountains along the border between Arkansas and Oklahoma in the United States.', 'node_id': 'b7996e6d-27a2-432b-8a46-88862f4793a4', 'score': 7.391604900360107}], 'consistency_check': 'MISSING', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'NEUTRAL', 'response': 'ENTAILMENT'}, 'latency': 5.33517505787313, 'vram_usage': 0.0}
