 71%|███████   | 355/500 [58:35<17:59,  7.45s/it]['no'] {'response': 'The provided context is insufficient regarding Pieris. It only discusses Phlebodium as a genus of ferns, but does not provide any information about Pieris or its classification. Therefore, I cannot determine if Pieris is a species of ferns based on the given context.', 'query': 'Are Phlebodium and Pieris both species of ferns?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': 'Phlebodium is a small genus of two to four species of ferns, native to tropical and subtropical regions of the Americas. The genus is closely related to "Polypodium", and the species were formerly included in that genus.', 'node_id': '17ded326-0b6c-4ce6-bf1a-dea5eb627150', 'score': 0.394839346408844}, {'text': 'Fern species with short trunks in the genera "Blechnum, Calochleana, Cnemedaria, Culcita" (Europe\'s only tree fern), "Cystodium, Leptopteris, Lophosoria, Sadleria, Thyrsopteris" and "Todea" could also be considered tree ferns in a liberal interpretation of the term.', 'node_id': '78e36202-acbd-41f0-a8e3-e084532c76a1', 'score': 0.7256759405136108}, {'text': 'The leptosporangiate ferns are one of the four major groups of ferns, with the other three being the Eusporangiate ferns comprising the marattioid ferns (Marattiidae, Marattiaceae), the horsetails (Equisetiidae, Equisetaceae), and whisk ferns and moonworts.', 'node_id': '1344dc23-3e3f-402a-a045-ed8fd28f7c81', 'score': 0.7491835951805115}, {'text': 'Leptosporangiate ferns are the largest group of living ferns, including some 11000 species worldwide. They constitute the subclass Polypodiidae, but are often considered to be the class Pteridopsida or Polypodiopsida, although other classifications assign them a different rank.', 'node_id': '38d2d97f-e5f8-4655-9776-c3b51398f8be', 'score': 0.7530613541603088}, {'text': 'Their large and multiple pinnate fronds, with the petiole raised adaxially, and the hairs on the rhizome and lower part of the petioles, also resemble those of tree ferns. To identify the species, use the position and characteristics of the spores found on the fertile fronds. The genus already existed in the Cretaceous Period in southern Gondwana according to fossil remains found in Antarctica. The species is well known as an ornamental plant.', 'node_id': '460e9c16-c836-4aa9-b875-486bdd58c3a2', 'score': 0.7777777314186096}], 'consistency_check': 'MISSING', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'NEUTRAL', 'response': 'NEUTRAL'}, 'latency': 0.7137858662754297, 'vram_usage': 1.17724609375}
