['no'] {'response': 'No, Phlebodium is a genus of ferns, while Pieris is a genus of butterflies in the family Pieridae. The context specifies that Phlebodium is a small genus of ferns native to tropical and subtropical regions of the Americas, whereas Pieris refers to butterflies, including species such as the Indian cabbage white (Pieris canidia) and the common cabbage butterfly (Pieris rapae). Therefore, while Phlebodium is indeed a fern, Pieris is not a fern but rather a group of butterflies.', 'query': 'Are Phlebodium and Pieris both species of ferns?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': 'Phlebodium is a small genus of two to four species of ferns, native to tropical and subtropical regions of the Americas. The genus is closely related to "Polypodium", and the species were formerly included in that genus.', 'node_id': '17ded326-0b6c-4ce6-bf1a-dea5eb627150', 'score': 10.208290100097656}, {'text': 'There are species of "Pieris" that are not pests, such as the North American species "Pieris oleracea" and "Pieris virginiensis". These butterflies feed successfully only on specific native vegetation.', 'node_id': 'b4d8d4d4-94bd-45b5-91fb-48fc1e37aa19', 'score': 8.412456512451172}, {'text': 'Phlebodium aureum (golden polypody, golden serpent fern, cabbage palm fern, gold-foot fern, hare-foot fern; syn. "Polypodium aureum", "Polypodium leucatomos") is an epiphytic fern native to tropical and subtropical regions of the Americas. It is confined to the eastern side of the continents, extending north into the United States to Florida and the extreme southeast of Georgia, and south through the Caribbean (the Bahamas, Puerto Rico, and Lesser Antilles), and northern and eastern South America to Paraguay.', 'node_id': 'ac2bba78-9922-4d4d-9a3c-fe3ce5d9454c', 'score': 7.404684066772461}, {'text': 'Pieris canidia, the Indian cabbage white, is a butterfly in the family Pieridae. "Pieris rapae" is one of the closest species in the Pieridae family.', 'node_id': '2c042a30-8511-4b86-932a-666cc013035f', 'score': 7.355023384094238}, {'text': 'The andromeda lace bug (Stephanitis takeyai) is a pest insect on plants of the genus "Pieris", especially "Pieris japonica", the Japanese andromeda. It originated in Japan with its host plant but has since been introduced to other areas of the globe. At least one "Pieris" species, "Pieris floribunda", is resistant to the bug.', 'node_id': 'cc8b76db-d578-4d61-a159-73fa106c0f66', 'score': 7.0057830810546875}, {'text': 'Fern species with short trunks in the genera "Blechnum, Calochleana, Cnemedaria, Culcita" (Europe\'s only tree fern), "Cystodium, Leptopteris, Lophosoria, Sadleria, Thyrsopteris" and "Todea" could also be considered tree ferns in a liberal interpretation of the term.', 'node_id': '78e36202-acbd-41f0-a8e3-e084532c76a1', 'score': 6.718524932861328}, {'text': 'It is the only species of "Phlebodium" found in North America, the other species all confined to South America. Other common names include calaguala (Spanish language), laua`e haole (Hawaiian), samambaia (Portuguese), and hartassbräken (Swedish).', 'node_id': '9a4db44d-122a-47ce-829e-369f4a8c188a', 'score': 6.554964065551758}, {'text': 'Leptosporangiate ferns are the largest group of living ferns, including some 11000 species worldwide. They constitute the subclass Polypodiidae, but are often considered to be the class Pteridopsida or Polypodiopsida, although other classifications assign them a different rank.', 'node_id': '38d2d97f-e5f8-4655-9776-c3b51398f8be', 'score': 6.233209609985352}, {'text': 'Diamondleaf fern is a common name. In Spanish it is known as \'ampe\' (from the Mapudungun añpe) or palmilla, but one has to remember that there are several species of ferns called "palmillas" that have larger or smaller fronds, and which grow in colder climates.', 'node_id': 'e4f380bf-a218-4b15-a89c-8def44548d80', 'score': 6.170536994934082}, {'text': 'In contrast to most other insects, "Pieris" caterpillars are able to detoxify these chemicals, and have become so specialised that they will not eat any food without glucosinolates. The "Pieris" females, in turn, check for the presence of glucosinolates before laying eggs on a plant. The crop-damaging species have spread from Eurasia to most of the rest of the world (most recently to South America and Africa) and are considered pest insects almost everywhere.', 'node_id': '795c7def-234f-412b-87fd-a85d8db6d8b6', 'score': 5.35340690612793}], 'consistency_check': 'MISSING', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'CONTRADICTION', 'response': 'ENTAILMENT'}, 'latency': 5.4100779835134745, 'vram_usage': 0.0}
