 78%|███████▊  | 388/500 [1:04:43<22:35, 12.10s/it]['middle America'] {'response': 'Yes, the man associated with the award that "Summer of the Monkeys" won is William Allen White. He was an American newspaper editor, politician, author, and leader of the Progressive movement. Between 1896 and his death in 1944, White became a spokesman for middle America, which can be interpreted as representing the white sector of America during that time. Therefore, it is accurate to say that he was a spokesman for that demographic.', 'query': 'The man, who the award in which "Summer of the Monkeys" won, was a spokesman for white sector of America?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': "Summer of the Monkeys is a 1976 children's novel written by Wilson Rawls. It was published by Doubleday (later released by Yearling Books) and was the winner of the William Allen White Book Award and the California Young Reader Medal.", 'node_id': '19a32153-6ae3-42c4-897c-a4be6b1e9aa2', 'score': 0.7599483728408813}, {'text': 'Scott Parkin (born 1969, Garland, Texas is an anti-war, environmental and global justice organizer, former community college history instructor, and a founding member of the Houston Global Awareness Collective. He has been a vocal critic of the American invasion of Iraq, and of corporations such as Exxonmobil and Halliburton. Since 2006, he has worked as an campaigner for the Rainforest Action Network, organizing campaigns against Bank of America, Citibank, TXU and the Keystone XL Pipeline. He also organizes with Rising Tide North America.', 'node_id': '9cc0c2c8-d70d-4326-a214-50057cb4d446', 'score': 1.1991697549819946}, {'text': 'William Allen White (February 10, 1868 – January 29, 1944) was an American newspaper editor, politician, author, and leader of the Progressive movement. Between 1896 and his death, White became a spokesman for middle America.', 'node_id': 'b23e0134-ab42-4db9-a894-23fdf98c3f07', 'score': 1.2031633853912354}, {'text': 'in History from UCLA in 1993. In February 1998 he completed his PhD thesis in Comparative History at Brandeis University, and in the summer 2004 was appointed to the position of Associate Professor of History at Yeshiva University in New York City. Zimmerman is an American author proficient in Yiddish, Polish, Hebrew, Russian, and French.<ref name="yu.edu/cv"> </ref>', 'node_id': 'be0acfbb-0adb-4219-94ad-55927072a0eb', 'score': 1.2402784824371338}, {'text': 'Wendell Erdman Berry (born August 5, 1934) is an American novelist, poet, environmental activist, cultural critic, and farmer. A prolific author, he has written many novels, short stories, poems, and essays. He is an elected member of the Fellowship of Southern Writers, a recipient of The National Humanities Medal, and the Jefferson Lecturer for 2012. He is also a 2013 Fellow of The American Academy of Arts and Sciences. Berry was named the recipient of the 2013 Richard C. Holbrooke Distinguished Achievement Award.', 'node_id': '552d3e1f-c66e-49fc-a700-58f71dccfc6a', 'score': 1.2433732748031616}], 'consistency_check': 'CONFLICT', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'ENTAILMENT', 'response': 'ENTAILMENT'}, 'latency': 1.3328407034277916, 'vram_usage': 1.31787109375}
