 79%|███████▊  | 393/500 [1:05:51<25:36, 14.36s/it]['Essex'] {'response': 'The provided context is insufficient regarding the burial location of King Harold Godwinson.', 'query': 'In which county is the English king Harold Godwinson buried?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': "Tostig Godwinson ( 1026 – 25 September 1066) was an Anglo-Saxon Earl of Northumbria and brother of King Harold Godwinson. After being exiled by his brother, Tostig supported the Norwegian king Harald Hardrada's invasion of England, and was killed at the Battle of Stamford Bridge.", 'node_id': 'dd12194c-71bd-49ef-87ee-a1e49e2984fa', 'score': 0.7751569747924805}, {'text': 'Leofwine Godwinson (c. 1035 – 14 October 1066) was a younger brother of King Harold Godwinson, the fifth son of Earl Godwin.', 'node_id': 'ecf7ffab-b878-4e92-950f-cf7569def985', 'score': 0.7813867330551147}, {'text': 'Godwin of Wessex (Old English: "Godƿin" ; 100115 April 1053) was one of the most powerful earls in England under the Danish king Cnut the Great and his successors. Cnut made him the first Earl of Wessex. Godwin was the father of King Harold Godwinson and Edith of Wessex, wife of King Edward the Confessor.', 'node_id': '2cc41633-de85-4eac-9c77-f2f72bbb4e09', 'score': 0.8713623285293579}, {'text': 'Fictional accounts based on the events surrounding Harold Godwinson\'s brief reign as king of England have been published, notably the play "Harold", by Alfred, Lord Tennyson, in 1876; and the novel "Last of the Saxon Kings", by Edward Bulwer-Lytton, in 1848. Rudyard Kipling wrote a short story, included in his 1910 collection, "Rewards and Fairies", where an aged King Harold meets Henry I and dies in the arms of a Saxon knight.', 'node_id': 'c1623e63-ea7e-403e-81fd-89ec2df4dc13', 'score': 1.0227453708648682}, {'text': "The Battle of Stamford Bridge took place at the village of Stamford Bridge, East Riding of Yorkshire, in England on 25 September 1066, between an English army under King Harold Godwinson and an invading Norwegian force led by King Harald Hardrada and the English king's brother Tostig Godwinson. After a bloody battle, both Hardrada and Tostig along with most of the Norwegians were killed. Although Harold Godwinson repelled the Norwegian invaders, his army was defeated by the Normans at Hastings less than three weeks later.", 'node_id': '089db3d0-5499-46d0-ad8a-42ad9c84e339', 'score': 1.0327166318893433}], 'consistency_check': 'CONFLICT', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'NEUTRAL', 'response': 'NEUTRAL'}, 'latency': 0.6599712334573269, 'vram_usage': 1.17724609375}
