 93%|█████████▎| 465/500 [1:17:02<06:06, 10.46s/it] 93%|█████████▎| 466/500 [1:17:06<04:48,  8.50s/it]['Sanskrit'] {'response': 'The provided context is insufficient regarding the connection between elephants and Gajabrishta.', 'query': 'How are elephants connected to Gajabrishta?', 'retrieved_context': [{'text': 'It has also been attributed to the true story of Olga the elephant rescued from Vienna Zoo in 1944. The title is a reference to the Carthaginian military commander Hannibal who led an army of war elephants over the Alps.', 'node_id': '08a41223-b1a1-4b0b-a204-2b2a95cd1226', 'score': 1.1230738162994385}, {'text': "However, the lead elephant, known as 'Mahmud' (Arabic: مَـحْـمُـوْد\u200e \u200e , consonant letters: m-ħ-m-w-d), is said to have stopped at the boundary around Mecca, and refused to enter. It has been theorized that an epidemic such as by smallpox could have caused such a failed invasion of Mecca.", 'node_id': 'c509daa5-fb96-4283-b531-8c706ad16768', 'score': 1.1718173027038574}, {'text': 'The tooth morphology, when combined with tooth wear and orientation, suggests that this animal may have been an omnivore or herbivore. Additionally, the gracile limbs and their orientation imply that this was a terrestrial animal. These features demonstrate that "Araripesuchus wegeneri" stands in stark contrast to its modern-day relatives, crocodiles and alligators.', 'node_id': '457b0d22-9683-4d66-b40a-0d54a3c6dcf7', 'score': 1.1982052326202393}, {'text': 'To Shoot an Elephant is a 2009 documentary film about the 2008-2009 Gaza War directed by Alberto Arce and Mohammad Rujailahk.', 'node_id': '15b69561-1ecf-4b4f-b9b0-b460d29a111b', 'score': 1.1983599662780762}, {'text': 'The North African elephant ("Loxodonta africana pharaoensis") was the subspecies of the African bush elephant ("Loxodonta africana"), or possibly a separate elephant species, that existed in North Africa north of the Sahara until becoming extinct in Roman times. These were the famous war elephants used by Carthage in the Punic Wars, their conflict with the Roman Republic. Although the subspecies has been formally described, it has not been widely recognized by taxonomists. Other names for this animal include the North African forest elephant, Carthaginian elephant, and Atlas elephant.', 'node_id': '2d244cd7-e0f8-4481-9a09-681c6fab2ad3', 'score': 1.2352900505065918}], 'consistency_check': 'MISSING', 'entailment_check': {'query': 'ENTAILMENT', 'response': 'NEUTRAL'}, 'latency': 0.691078582778573, 'vram_usage': 1.15380859375}
